1
00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.436
Welcome to Getting Structured LLM Output

2
00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:06.239
built in partnership with DotTxt.

3
00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:07.340
When

4
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.577
working with LLMs in a typical chat
interface, unstructured

5
00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:13.580
text output is fine,
but if you're building software,

6
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.351
it becomes much harder to parse
and rely on free-form text output.

7
00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:22.355
That's where structured output
such as JSON becomes very important.

8
00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:26.593
They provide a clear format
to the machine can read and process.

9
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.494
For example,

10
00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:31.331
if you want your LLM to look at a product
review and generate a couple of fields,

11
00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:34.734
say the product name and whether
the sentiment is positive or negative,

12
00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:39.239
then outputting those two fields in JSON
that's downstream software reads

13
00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:42.242
that process these fields reliably.

14
00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:42.909
In this course,

15
00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:46.413
you will learn how to obtain structured outputs
using several approaches.

16
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.150
The basic idea is you can tell an LLM
that you would like the data

17
00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:54.287
in a particular format, and if you write the format
clearly, specifically

18
00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:58.858
using what computer scientists
call a regex or regular expression.

19
00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:04.064
Then they'll be an efficient way to get an LLM to
reliably generate outputs in that format,

20
00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:07.534
because regex can be used to enforce
constraints on the next

21
00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:10.537
token to be generated one token at a time.

22
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:14.874
I'm delighted to introduce the instructors: Will Kurt and Cameron Pfiffer.

23
00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:18.344
Will, is a founding engineer at DotTxt,

24
00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:21.481
and Cameron is a developer
relations engineer.

25
00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:22.582
And they help developers

26
00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:27.187
like you to build and integrate reliable
structured formats into their LLM apps.

27
00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:28.755
Thanks, Andrew.

28
00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:29.689
In this course

29
00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:33.526
you will first get structured output from
models that support structured responses.

30
00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:36.930
You also learn about the limitations
of this approach.

31
00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.598
To address some of these limitations.

32
00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:42.035
We'll use Instructor,
an open source library that re-prompts

33
00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:45.038
the model until a valid JSON
structure is produced.

34
00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:48.108
You will also learn
how constrained decoding works.

35
00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:49.576
this is the underlying concept

36
00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:53.146
behind outlines, our open source library
that you will use in this course.

37
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.282
While learning all the concepts
in this course, you'll work on several

38
00:01:56.282 --> 00:02:00.520
cool examples and use cases,
including a social media analysis agent.

39
00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:02.355
The agent reads user posts,

40
00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:05.892
identifies the sentiment, and decides
whether or not a response is needed.

41
00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:09.195
You can even generate a customer
support ticket if there's an issue.

42
00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:10.830
All of the Json output.

43
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.833
After working with retry-based
methods, you'll learn how you can get

44
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:16.836
the structure and output from the get-go
with outlines,

45
00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.873
a library that constrains model outputs
at the token level

46
00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:23.877
by intercepting the logits
the probabilities the model assigns to

47
00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:25.845
each next token-Outlines blocks

48
00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:28.815
any tokens that don't fit your defined schema or format.

49
00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:32.385
This guarantees a valid output
without any do-overs.

50
00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:36.923
In the last lesson, you will learn
how you can generate beyond normal JSON.

51
00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:40.126
For example, you will learn
how to generate valid phone numbers,

52
00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:43.296
email addresses,
or even ASCII tic-tac-toe boards;

53
00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:46.432
and basically any format
you can express in a regex.

54
00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:49.402
Many people have worked to create this
course.

55
00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:52.238
I'd like to thank Rémi Louf and Andrea Pessl

56
00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:55.175
from DotTxt. From DeepLearning.AI,

57
00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:58.178
Esmaeil Gargari
has also contributed to this course.

58
00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:01.981
The first lesson will be an introduction
to structured output generation.

59
00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:03.483
That sounds great.

60
00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:06.419
Let's go on to the next video
and let's get structured.

